pyperl - Perl for Python
------------------------

This is a Python extension module that embeds Perl interpreter(s) in
any Python program.  It can be used to invoke arbitrary Perl code, load
any Perl modules and make calls directly into Perl functions.  The Perl
code invoked can call back into Python as it sees fit.

This is a maintained fork of pyperl 1.0.3 (originally written by Gisle
Aas at ActiveState): https://github.com/mgheorghe/pyperl

Requirements:

  - Python 3.6 or newer with its development headers (tested: 3.6 to 3.15)
  - Perl 5.10.1 or newer built with a shared libperl, with its development
    files (tested: 5.10.1 to 5.44); e.g. libperl-dev on Debian/Ubuntu,
    perl-devel and perl-ExtUtils-Embed on RHEL/Fedora
  - a C compiler and make
  - Linux, macOS, or Windows with Strawberry Perl (its gcc builds pyperl)

Free-threaded Python builds work: importing perl re-enables the GIL
(Python prints a RuntimeWarning), and the import fails if the GIL was
forced off with PYTHON_GIL=0 or -X gil=0.

Install (with 'perl' on your PATH being the perl to embed):

    pip install .

or straight from GitHub:

    pip install git+https://github.com/mgheorghe/pyperl

The Perl side (Python::Object) is installed next to the extension, so
nothing is added to perl's own library.  On Windows a pyperl-dll.pth
file is installed as well; it lets Python find perl5xx.dll.

Two flavours can be built.  With the MULTI_PERL file present (the
default in this repository) each Python thread gets its own perl
interpreter; this needs a perl built with ithreads (-Dusethreads) and
is skipped automatically for perls without them.  Delete MULTI_PERL to
share one interpreter between all threads.

Run the tests from a source tree:

    python setup.py build
    export PYTHONPATH=$(ls -d build/lib.*)
    python -m unittest discover -s tests -p '*.py'
    python test.py

The API is documented in perlmodule.pod ('perldoc perlmodule.pod').
Report bugs at https://github.com/mgheorghe/pyperl/issues

